    description: "WHAT\nA Rust function parameter written as a struct pattern hashes the names it binds: `fn f(Point { x: px, y }: Point)` and `fn f(Point { x: qx, y }: Point)` get two stable ids, while a typed parameter `p: Point` hashes its type alone. The jsts agent read the cause from code: the shared names-first signature normalizer splits a parameter at its first `:`, which inside a struct pattern is the field separator `x: px`, not the parameter's type annotation. PR #2354 fixed the same cut for JS/TS patterns (0f5db7682: a typed pattern hashes its type alone, an untyped pattern is hashed whole); Rust patterns were not touched.\n\nREPRO\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/v19_11232026/residuals/probes/rust_pattern/{a,b,c}/lib.rs, PR tip 3269081cf (drafting agent, 2026-11-24):\n  a: fn f(Point { x: px, y }: Point) -> i32   hgstableid19:597d560f7513baca\n  b: fn f(Point { x: qx, y }: Point) -> i32   hgstableid19:147001cdd5c83ee1\n  c: fn f(p: Point) -> i32                    hgstableid19:0f0ba73db7a3d482\na and b differ only in a bound name and hash apart.\n\nMEASURED\nFixture only. The jsts agent's statement was \"read from code, NOT run\"; the probe above is the first run. No corpus count of struct-pattern or tuple-struct-pattern parameters.\n\nNOT CHECKED\n- Tuple-struct patterns `Wrapper(inner): Wrapper`, tuple patterns `(a, b): (i32, i32)`, slice patterns, closures.\n- Which string the hash receives for a/b (the hashed text was not printed).\n- Other languages through the same normalizer (Kotlin, Scala, Swift take no pattern parameters; not checked for others).\n\nA fix changes the hashed text of every Rust callable with a pattern parameter: needs a scheme bump (owner-ruled).\n\nFound 2026-11-23/24 while building PR #2354 (stable-id v19). Detail: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/v19_11232026/STATUS.md line 119 (jsts agent's final message)"  # c0fb
